DF4  LRC(14): THM-4449's pair energy vs physical union mass, recomputed and generalized
====================================================================================================
  For odd tails no tail kills both lifts, so y fails iff K1, K2 are both nonempty, and the number of pairs
  {a,b} killing both lifts is |K1||K2|.  Hence, exactly, pair energy - union mass = E[(|K1||K2| - 1) 1_F].
    T=(1, 11, 121): mu(F_T) = 108/847 = 0.127509; E(T) = 124/847 = 0.146399; defect mu(Omega) = 16/847 (0.129 of E); any tail killing both lifts: False
    T=(1, 9, 23): mu(F_T) = 214/1449 = 0.147688; E(T) = 74/483 = 0.153209; defect mu(Omega) = 8/1449 (0.036 of E); any tail killing both lifts: False
    T=(1, 7, 11): mu(F_T) = 72/539 = 0.133581; E(T) = 72/539 = 0.133581; defect mu(Omega) = 0 (0.000 of E); any tail killing both lifts: False
    T=(3, 5, 7, 11): mu(F_T) = 1858/8085 = 0.229808; E(T) = 1942/8085 = 0.240198; defect mu(Omega) = 4/385 (0.043 of E); any tail killing both lifts: False
    T=(1, 9, 23, 37): mu(F_T) = 11876/53613 = 0.221513; E(T) = 15038/53613 = 0.280492; defect mu(Omega) = 1054/17871 (0.210 of E); any tail killing both lifts: False
  THM-4449 values: (1,11,121): E = 124/847 = 108/847 + 16/847; sharp caps mu(F) <= 214/1449 at (1,9,23) and
  72/539 at (1,7,11).  The defect is the (1,2)/(2,1) owner-count locus: an exactly calculable, structured part
  of the union bound, already harvested by THM-4449; the four-tail rows show it grows with the tail count.
====================================================================================================
DF5  dyadic shadows: GF(2) linear-complexity (Hankel) profiles of the lacunary skeletons
====================================================================================================
  A 0/1 sequence has all Hankel determinants odd iff its profile is perfect (L_i = ceil(i/2)), iff all
  partial quotients of its GF(2) continued fraction have degree 1.  Wang-Massey (1986, CITED, not re-read):
  s_1 = 1 and s_(2i+1) = s_(2i) + s_i characterizes perfect profiles; the powers-of-2 indicator satisfies it.
    powers of 2 (AMM skeleton, positions 1,2,4,...)  n=6000: perfect profile: True; max |L_i - ceil(i/2)| = 0; jump sizes (count): [(1, 2999)]
    squares (Collatz theta, positions 1,4,9,...)     n=6000: perfect profile: False; max |L_i - ceil(i/2)| = 6; jump sizes (count): [(1, 766), (2, 386), (3, 199), (4, 82), (5, 45), (6, 22)]
    cubes (Collatz Y3, positions 1,8,27,...)         n=6000: perfect profile: False; max |L_i - ceil(i/2)| = 5; jump sizes (count): [(1, 743), (2, 395), (3, 171), (4, 70), (5, 47), (6, 21)]
    i.i.d. fair bits (control)                       n=6000: perfect profile: False; max |L_i - ceil(i/2)| = 7; jump sizes (count): [(1, 690), (2, 365), (3, 192), (4, 108), (5, 44), (6, 22)]
  => the AMM parity skeleton is maximally structured over GF(2) (automatic; Artin-Schreier X^2+X = w);
     the Collatz theta skeletons are GF(2)-random-like (non-automatic; cf. HC-TH6 for cubes).  The shared
     'dyadic' label hides opposite behaviour: finite-state parity in AMM, no parity structure in Collatz.

