    n=28 (a=14) r=5: open clocks c <= 60: [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 12, 16]
  n=14, r=3 open signatures (valuation vectors of the three tails at the primes of c):
    c=2: [((0,), (0,), (0,)), ((0,), (0,), (1,))]
    c=3: [((0,), (0,), (0,))]
    c=4: [((0,), (0,), (1,))]
  THM-4447 (13): c=2 zero or one even tail; c=3 no tail divisible by 3; c=4 exactly one tail with v_2 = 1 and
  two odd tails.  Reproduced exactly above.
  => the open clocks are the small integers where r tails of capacity ceil(q/a) (boosted by gcd) can still
     cover q labels; they move with r and a.  The '3' in LRC(14) is r = 3 tails (13 = 10 + 3), not a
     2-versus-3 transcendence seam: no logarithm, no 2^K - 3^L and no Baker-type quantity enters.
====================================================================================================
BR2  the Collatz side: HYP-9122's finite form and its Diophantine skeleton
====================================================================================================
  K0(s) = floor((s+1) log_2 3) is the minimal number of halvings of a loop through 1 with s multiplications
  (PROVED lower bound; HYP-9122 says it is attained for every s; FINITE-EXACT for s <= 6000).
    s=  2: K0 = 4, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 1.777778 (in (3/2, 3))
    s=  3: K0 = 6, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 2.370370 (in (3/2, 3))
    s=  4: K0 = 7, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 1.580247 (in (3/2, 3))
    s=  8: K0 = 14, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 2.497180 (in (3/2, 3))
    s= 16: K0 = 26, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 1.558977 (in (3/2, 3))
    s= 40: K0 = 64, loop ratio 2^K0/3^s = 1.517293 (in (3/2, 3))
  record denominators (upper best approximations of log_2 3, 2^p/3^q -> 1+): [1, 3, 5, 17, 29, 41, 94, 147, 200, 253, 306, 971, 1636, 2301, 2966]
  (HYP-9122 lists 3, 5, 17, 29, 41, 94, 147, 200, 253, 306, 971, 1636, 2301, ...; the S596 'two-block'
  2^E - 3^k enters exactly here.)  The record ratios: [1.333333, 1.185185, 1.053498, 1.039318, 1.025329, 1.011529, 1.009419, 1.007313, 1.005212]
  Contrast: the Collatz clock set is infinite and governed by the continued fraction of the transcendental
  log_2 3 (linear forms in logarithms); the LRC(14) clock set (BR1) is finite and pigeonhole-determined.
====================================================================================================
BR3  extremal profiles are mechanical words with logarithmic slopes
====================================================================================================
  gamma* = log_5 phi^2 (AMM THM-3009 Beatty profile)   = 0.59798743566544  CF [0, 1, 1, 2, 19, 2, 9, 1, 1, 3, 1, 9, 1, 2]
  C_* = 1 + gamma*                                     = 1.59798743566544  CF [1, 1, 1, 2, 19, 2, 9, 1, 1, 3, 1, 9, 1, 2]
  log_3 2 (Collatz critical odd density)               = 0.630929753571457  CF [0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 5, 2, 23, 2, 2, 1]
  log_2 3 (Collatz clocks)                             = 1.58496250072116  CF [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1, 5, 2, 23, 2, 2, 1, 1]
  convergents of C_*: ['1', '2', '3/2', '8/5', '155/97', '318/199', '3017/1888']
  exact AMM finite-block optima (uniform frontier sec. 6.1/abstract): ['14/9', '25/16', '53/34', '83/53', '157/100'] -> none is a convergent of C_*: True
  Both extremal profiles are Beatty words of slopes log(alpha)/log(beta) (transcendental by Gelfond-Schneider).
  In Collatz the slope's continued fraction is the whole cycle problem (Baker, BR2); in AMM the slope enters
  only through floor rounding of the deadline and no Diophantine obstruction attached to it is known: shared
  toolbox (Beatty/Ostrowski/three-distance, also LRC THM-536/778), ANALOGY only.
